mary":"Article reporting the discovery of an unlooted Bronze Age warrior’s tomb 
near the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos, Pylos. It describes the 
tomb’s context and major finds: four solid-gold rings (including one with a 
Cretan bull-jumping scene), more than four dozen seal stones with intricate 
Minoan designs, six ivory combs, a long gold necklace with ivy-leaf pendants 
found near the warrior’s neck, a bronze mirror with ivory handle, and over 1,400
objects in total (jewels, weapons, armor, and metal vessels). The tomb dates to 
about 1500 B.C. and was excavated by a University of Cincinnati team led by Jack
L. Davis and Sharon R. 
Stocker.","source_ref":"s1"},{"local_ref":"d2","document_summary":"Museum 
exhibition notice describing \"The Pylos of Nestor. A Mycenaean Kingdom 
Revealed\" at the Hellenic National Archaeological Museum. It outlines the 
exhibition’s temporal span and thematic focus: tracing the emergence of the 
Mycenaean kingdom centered on the Palace at Ano Englianos, traditionally 
associated with Homeric Nestor. The exhibition uses burial monuments with rich 
grave goods, prestigious objects, and administrative records to illustrate 
wealth, social organization, ideology, and the rise of the wanax’s authority, as
well as Messenia’s connections to the Minoan world, wider Mycenaean sphere, and 
Eastern Mediterranean. It emphasizes archaeological documentation (excavation 
diaries, drawings, archival photographs) and follows a museographic narrative 
from discovery in the field to analysis in a lab-like 
setting.","source_ref":"s2"},{"local_ref":"d3","document_summary":"Scholarly 
article introducing the Pylos Regional Archaeological Project (PRAP), a 
systematic investigation of prehistoric and historical settlement and land use 
in western Messenia centered on the Palace of Nestor. It describes the survey 
area (around 40 km² intensively examined plus about 30 km² of previously known 
sites), methods of intensive surface survey and environmental studies 
(geological, geomorphological, geophysical, paleobotanical), and the project’s 
goal of defining sites’ spatial extent and chronological components. It situates
PRAP in the context of prior work on Mycenaean geography, regional organization,
and excavations near Pylos, noting that many sites in the Palace of Nestor’s 
territory during Late Helladic IIIB lay under palace 
control.","source_ref":"s3"},{"local_ref":"d4","document_summary":"Short 
encyclopedia-style page summarizing archaeologist Sharon Stocker’s career. It 
mentions that her team discovered two previously unknown Mycenaean tholos tombs 
(Tholos VI and VII) in May 2018 in the Demopoulos Field near the Palace of 
Nestor, and refers to her work on the Griffin Warrior Tomb and related 
finds.","source_ref":"s4"}],"statements":[{"local_ref":"st1","text":"An unlooted
