[AIMessage → tool] create_analysis({'text': 'Synthesis for article planning: The
Ring of Nestor is treated in modern scholarship as a gold signet ring acquired 
and documented by Arthur Evans, usually framed as coming from a Mycenaean tholos
tomb context and used extensively in discussions of Minoan–Mycenaean religion, 
iconography, and authenticity/forgery debates. However, the precise provenance 
remains disputed: some authors mention Kakovatos, others Mycenae, and there is 
no secure stratigraphic record comparable to recent finds such as the Griffin 
Warrior tomb. For article purposes, the lead and context sections should present
this uncertainty explicitly, attributing each claimed provenance to the relevant
scholars and avoiding categorical statements about the findspot. The ring’s 
physical description will have to rely on Evans’s drawings and later 
iconographic studies; measurements and catalogue data appear fragmentary, so the
article should state only what can be sourced, without speculating. 
Archaeological context should distinguish between the Evans-associated ring 
(with uncertain provenance) and later elite burials near the Palace of Nestor 
that have produced other gold rings, to avoid conflating assemblages. Debates 
about authenticity and iconographic meaning (including Nilsson’s doubts, later 
defenses, and use of the ring as a gemma dubitanda) should be summarized with 
clear attribution and due weight, noting that the object has become a case study
in Aegean forgery discussions rather than an uncontested reference artifact.', 
'query_id': '20260628003916290672_13064f87'})
