submit_final_report({'title': 'Ring of Nestor', 'sections': [{'title': 'Lead', 
'blocks': [{'text': 'The **Ring of Nestor** is the conventional modern name for 
a gold signet ring attributed to the Aegean Bronze Age and closely associated 
with the archaeologist Arthur J. Evans, who acquired, studied, and illustrated 
the object in the early 20th century.[1][10][13][21][28] The ring is generally 
treated in scholarship as a high‑status item of Aegean glyptic art whose 
iconography includes cult imagery and a composite creature that has been 
described as a “Minoan dragon,” and it has been repeatedly cited in discussions 
of Minoan–Mycenaean religion and the adoption of Minoan cult symbols on the 
Greek mainland.[8][9][12][15] The object’s authenticity and provenance are 
disputed: Evans accepted it as genuine, while some later scholars classified it 
among suspect or possibly forged artefacts, and other researchers have argued in
favor of its authenticity on the basis of new comparative material and 
iconographic analysis.[1][2][7][10][26][28][31] Because the Ring of Nestor lacks
a securely documented excavation context and has been variously linked in 
secondary literature to sites such as Kakovatos, Mycenae, Thisbê in Boeotia, and
the region of Pylos, it has become a case study in the interpretive challenges 
posed by Aegean sealstones and signet rings of uncertain provenance, sometimes 
discussed under the category *gemmae dubitandae*.[1][8][19][21][32][34][35]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628003951047343_67076a76', '20260628004021300624_ad4df5cc',
'20260628004021382516_eab88ade', '20260628004614325301_60cc1b53', 
'20260628004916760676_d08416e9', '20260628004021277210_78e5b375', 
'20260628004021354207_3f325615', '20260628004040270204_142efbb2', 
'20260628004021222852_03fa4b94', '20260628003951064180_4a29628f', 
'20260628004916680829_39f17228', '20260628004935379106_4118c2e3', 
'20260628004935413586_5acc7279', '20260628004935471509_5a154c36', 
'20260628004935498281_cbdf50b2'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}], 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628003951806867_7d3ed7f5', 
'20260628004022645094_27430b01', '20260628004917424487_fa1c1aef', 
'20260628004936830153_b95d8a4b'], 'section_type': 'overview'}, {'title': 
'Discovery and provenance', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Arthur J. Evans published a 
study entitled “The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia” in
the *Journal of Hellenic Studies*, in which he discussed a group of gold signet 
rings and bead seals said to come from a sepulchral treasure at Thisbê.[21][34] 
The Heidelberg catalogue entry for this publication describes it as a London 
work of 1925 on the Thisbê treasure, indicating that Evans framed the ring 
within a Boeotian context rather than as an excavated object from a documented 
tomb at a single site.[21][34]', 'source_ids': ['20260628004614325301_60cc1b53',
'20260628004935471509_5a154c36'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later 
secondary literature presents conflicting accounts of the ring’s provenance. A 
Royal Holloway thesis, citing Evans’s *Journal of Hellenic Studies* article, 
states that the Ring of Nestor was found in Chamber Tomb 58 at Mycenae.[32] A 
separate MA thesis on dance identifies the “so‑called ring of Nestor” as CMS VI,
277, describes it as having been found at “the tomb of Nestor’s Pylos,” and 
associates its decoration with that setting.[35] Other modern discussions treat 
the ring as part of the treasure from Thisbê in Boeotia, as framed in Evans’s 
article.[21][34] These sources collectively show that the ring has been linked 
to Mycenae, Pylos, and Thisbê in different scholarly contexts, without a single 
universally accepted provenance.[32][34][35]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628004935413586_5acc7279', '20260628004935498281_cbdf50b2', 
'20260628004614325301_60cc1b53'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Further 
complication arises from references to Kakovatos in the wider literature on 
Bronze Age Greece. A chapter on the Bronze Age civilization of Greece notes that
from the tholos tomb at Kakovatos “apparently” comes a famous ring that Evans 
acquired, the “ring of Nestor,” and adds that its authenticity is disputed.[10] 
A later study by A. G. Vlachopoulos reports that Evans purchased and studied the
ring, gifted it to the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford, and was persuaded that it had
been found in Tholos Tomb A at Kakovatos.[28] These statements show that 
Kakovatos has been presented as the ring’s presumed findspot in some modern 
accounts, though the wording “apparently” and “persuaded” signals that this 
association reflects Evans’s belief rather than a securely documented excavation
context.[10][28]', 'source_ids': ['20260628004021300624_ad4df5cc', 
'20260628004916760676_d08416e9'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Arthur Evans’s
biographers note that he drew the Ring of Nestor in his Knossos notebooks, with 
one drawing now preserved in the Ashmolean Museum (Oxford, notebook 42, pp. 
82–83, Box 10), indicating his direct engagement with the object and its 
subsequent housing in a museum collection.[13] A popular article on the Griffin 
Warrior tomb at Pylos remarks that a gold signet ring from Tholos Tomb IV is the
only excavated gold signet ring from that area and that “the only other from 
this area was the famous Ring of Nestor – purchased by Arthur Evans,” implying 
that the Ring of Nestor was acquired on the art market rather than excavated in 
situ under stratigraphically controlled conditions.[36] Taken together, these 
accounts show that while Evans studied and publicized the ring, the surviving 
evidence does not supply a single secure excavation context; instead, the ring’s
provenance has been variously reported as Thisbê, Kakovatos, Mycenae, or Pylos 
and is widely regarded as uncertain.[13][21][28][32][35][36]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628004021382516_eab88ade', '20260628004614325301_60cc1b53', 
'20260628004916760676_d08416e9', '20260628004935413586_5acc7279', 
'20260628004935498281_cbdf50b2', '20260628004935530099_508d9228'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Modern analytical works therefore treat the Ring of 
Nestor as an artefact without secure archaeological context. Hudler’s study of 
*gemmae dubitandae* notes that objects such as the Ring of Nestor illustrate how
artefacts whose archaeological properties are uncertain can nevertheless 
continue to be discussed and reinterpreted, and emphasizes that their alleged 
Bronze Age date is explicitly regarded as uncertain.[1] Palmer’s thesis on 
religious motifs in Aegean glyptic refers to the Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, 277) as
a problematic object that, along with another Ashmolean ring, has been 
“denounced,” and stresses that its alleged provenance in Mycenae needs to be 
treated with caution when interpreting its religious imagery.[8]', 'source_ids':
['20260628003951047343_67076a76', '20260628004021247324_ba69cb65'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628004917424487_fa1c1aef', 
'20260628004936830153_b95d8a4b'], 'section_type': 'analysis'}, {'title': 
'Description and iconography', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor is 
consistently described in scholarship as a gold signet ring, catalogued in the 
*Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel* as CMS VI, 277.[8][32][35] 
Although the evidence consulted here does not provide precise measurements, 
weight, or a detailed technical description, discussions of the ring treat it as
a large, high‑status item comparable to major Mycenaean signet rings, and note 
that its bezel bears a complex figural scene.[2][8][14][32]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628004021247324_ba69cb65', '20260628004040246292_f34020e9', 
'20260628003951064180_4a29628f', '20260628004935413586_5acc7279', 
'20260628004935498281_cbdf50b2'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A contribution
in the Aegeus Society’s *Newsletter on Aegean Prehistory* introduces 
Eliopoulos’s study “Some observations on the iconography of the ‘Ring of 
Nestor’” by noting that “the bezel of a gold signet ring was found during the 
excavation of an” unspecified context and that this bezel is referred to as the 
Ring of Nestor, indicating that the discussion focuses on the imagery engraved 
on the ring’s bezel.[29] The Academia.edu entry for Eliopoulos’s article 
characterizes it as exploring the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor through 
iconographic analysis, confirming that the study is centered on detailed 
examination of the scene rather than on metrics or metallurgical 
composition.[2][29][30]', 'source_ids': ['20260628004925233210_dce33685', 
'20260628003951064180_4a29628f', '20260628004934076549_c8303ca2'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'One strand of interpretation, represented by the study 
of composite creatures on seals and sealings, describes the main figure on the 
Ring of Nestor as a “Minoan Dragon” shown in a natural setting, “standing on a 
grassy ground in front of the trunk of the tree whose branches” extend above.[9]
This description identifies the principal motif as a composite creature 
interacting with a stylized tree, set on ground that is indicated by engraved 
vegetation, and shows that scholars have considered the creature’s form and 
setting significant for understanding hybrid beings in Aegean iconography.[9]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628004021277210_78e5b375'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 
'Other studies situate the Ring of Nestor’s scene within a broader repertoire of
cult imagery on Aegean seals and rings. Nilsson’s work on Minoan–Mycenaean 
religion includes the Ring of Nestor among artefacts whose iconography is “of 
importance for the subject,” suggesting that the ring’s scene was considered 
relevant to arguments about ritual, epiphany, or cult practice.[12] Palmer’s 
thesis on religious glyptic motifs notes that the Ring of Nestor’s imagery has 
been used in discussions of Minoan cult symbol adoption on the mainland, and 
compares its scene with those on securely provenanced rings such as CMS I, 126 
from Chamber Tomb 91 at Mycenae, which features a central cult figure, and the 
large signet ring CMS I, 179 from the Tiryns treasure.[14][15] These comparative
treatments underline that the Ring of Nestor’s iconography is structurally 
similar to established Late Bronze Age cult scenes, even though the ring’s own 
archaeological context remains uncertain.[8][12][14][15]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628004021354207_3f325615', '20260628004040270204_142efbb2', 
'20260628004040246292_f34020e9', '20260628004021247324_ba69cb65'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Because detailed measurements, manufacturing technique, 
and condition reports for the Ring of Nestor are not present in the accessible 
material, the ring’s physical description in current scholarship focuses on its 
material (gold), its classification as a signet ring with an engraved bezel, and
the composition of its figural scene, rather than on exact dimensions or 
technical analyses.[2][8][19]', 'source_ids': ['20260628003951064180_4a29628f', 
'20260628004021247324_ba69cb65', '20260628004614253695_ed7a6a43'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': [], 'section_type': 'analysis'}, 
{'title': 'Archaeological and historical context', 'blocks': [{'text': 'In 
general terms, the Ring of Nestor is situated by scholars within the tradition 
of Aegean Bronze Age glyptic, particularly Mycenaean and Minoan signet rings 
that bear cult scenes and composite creatures.[8][9][12][14][15] Nilsson’s 
survey of Minoan–Mycenaean religion lists the Ring of Nestor among key artefacts
used to reconstruct Bronze Age ritual and belief, indicating that its imagery 
has been integrated into wider arguments about religion in Crete and mainland 
Greece.[12] Studies of cult symbols and seal images place it alongside rings 
from Mycenae and Tiryns, treating its scene as part of a repertoire of elite 
religious iconography, even while acknowledging that its archaeological context 
is not securely documented.[8][14][15]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628004021354207_3f325615', '20260628004021247324_ba69cb65', 
'20260628004040246292_f34020e9', '20260628004040270204_142efbb2', 
'20260628004021277210_78e5b375'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Palmer’s 
analysis of Late Bronze Age Aegean glyptic motifs stresses that the Ring of 
Nestor (CMS VI, 277) has been “denounced” in modern scholarship, and notes that 
its alleged provenance in Mycenae implies a mainland context rather than a 
Cretan one.[8] On this basis, Palmer argues that the ring “does not refer to 
Neopalatial rituals,” framing its imagery as part of Mycenaean religious 
practice that appropriated Minoan cult symbols rather than as a direct 
representation of Cretan rites.[8] This approach places the ring conceptually 
within debates about how Mycenaean elites adopted, reinterpreted, or transformed
iconographic elements originally developed in Minoan cult settings.[8][15]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628004021247324_ba69cb65'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 
'Comparative studies emphasize that the Ring of Nestor cannot be treated in 
isolation from securely provenanced assemblages of Mycenaean rings. Work on the 
Griffin Warrior grave at Pylos describes an undisturbed Late Helladic II burial 
containing four gold signet rings with detailed Minoan‑style iconography, found 
in incontrovertible association with a single individual.[16][17][24][25] The 
excavators and subsequent commentators underline that the concentration of many 
gold rings with one burial is unexpected and unprecedented for the Greek 
mainland, and that the iconography of these rings is “of great importance for 
the study of Minoan and Mycenaean ideology in the early phases of the Late 
Bronze Age.”[16][17][24][25] These Pylos rings, whose stratigraphic context is 
well documented, provide a securely dated benchmark for early Mycenaean use of 
Minoan‑style imagery, and are frequently used as comparanda when assessing more 
problematic objects such as the Ring of Nestor.[8][14][24][25][36]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628004040294589_4ef25f33', '20260628004040318065_48743760',
'20260628004614385335_4ef25f33', '20260628004614407338_95dc4f2c', 
'20260628004935530099_508d9228', '20260628004040246292_f34020e9', 
'20260628004021247324_ba69cb65'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Similarly, 
rings from Grave Circle A at Mycenae and from chamber tombs in the same area 
have long formed part of the core dataset for Mycenaean glyptic. Hesperia 
reports describe a gold ring from Shaft Grave IV in Grave Circle A as having 
particularly detailed and refined iconography and show that such rings come from
stratified contexts within elite burials at Mycenae.[22] Cambridge’s treatment 
of the Late Helladic II period mentions additional gold signet rings of 
Mycenaean style from a chamber tomb at Mycenae, underlining that multiple rings 
from that tomb can be securely placed in the Mycenaean sequence.[20][22] These 
securely provenanced Mycenaean rings provide chronological and stylistic 
frameworks by which the Ring of Nestor’s imagery and typology have been 
assessed, even though the ring itself lacks comparable stratigraphic 
documentation.[8][20][22]', 'source_ids': ['20260628004614346547_9970cb77', 
'20260628004614304808_73bd9104', '20260628004021247324_ba69cb65'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Regional studies of Pylos and the Palace of Nestor 
further contextualize discussions of the Ring of Nestor. The Pylos Regional 
Archaeological Project outlines the development of the Pylian polity and the 
distribution of sites under palace control during the Late Helladic IIIB phase, 
around the twelfth century BC, providing a framework for understanding 
high‑status burials and religious imagery in the region.[5][19] Excavation and 
analysis of wall‑painting fragments from the Palace of Nestor show systematic 
cataloguing and study of iconographic material, although these works do not 
directly involve the Ring of Nestor.[22][23] Together with the Griffin Warrior 
burial, these studies define a securely stratified Mycenaean context at Pylos 
against which the unresolved provenance of the Ring of Nestor is often 
contrasted.[5][19][22][23][24][25][36]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628004001153521_221bfec1', '20260628004614253695_ed7a6a43', 
'20260628004614346547_9970cb77', '20260628004614365725_912859ed', 
'20260628004614385335_4ef25f33', '20260628004614407338_95dc4f2c', 
'20260628004935530099_508d9228'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}], 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628004002941672_c25a094d'], 'section_type': 
'analysis'}, {'title': 'Scholarly interpretations', 'blocks': [{'text': 'From 
the early 20th century onward, the Ring of Nestor has been the subject of 
changing scholarly assessments regarding dating, meaning, and authenticity. 
Evans’s original publication interpreted the ring within a sepulchral treasure 
from Thisbê in Boeotia and linked its imagery to the “Minoan after‑world,” 
implying a Bronze Age date and a religious or funerary significance connected to
Minoan beliefs.[21][34] Nilsson initially treated the ring as an authentic 
Bronze Age artefact in the first edition of his *Minoan‑Mycenaean Religion* and 
reproduced its illustration as a significant piece of evidence for 
reconstructing Minoan‑Mycenaean religious practice.[12] By the 1950 second 
edition, however, Nilsson reclassified the Ring of Nestor as a “suspect object,”
reflecting his growing concern over possible forgeries and the uncertain 
archaeological status of some glyptic items.[1][12]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628004614325301_60cc1b53', '20260628004935471509_5a154c36', 
'20260628004021354207_3f325615', '20260628003951047343_67076a76'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Mid‑20th‑century and later scholars expressed more 
explicit skepticism. Hudler’s analysis of *gemmae dubitandae* notes that in 1954
Hagen Biesantz “took a decisive stand against the ‘Ring of Nestor’s’ 
authenticity,” basing his judgment primarily on stylistic irregularities in the 
ring’s design.[1] Agni Xenaki‑Sakellariou’s 1994 study scrutinized the ring’s 
iconographical details, typology, and technical features and highlighted aspects
she regarded as suspicious, contributing to the view that the ring might not be 
a genuine Bronze Age artefact.[1] Hudler emphasizes that such critiques treat 
the ring’s alleged antiquity as explicitly uncertain while still engaging with 
its imagery and perceived significance.[1]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003951047343_67076a76'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Ingo Pini’s 
1998 article on the Ring of Nestor is described by Hudler as a direct response 
to Xenaki‑Sakellariou’s criticisms.[1] Pini is said to have referred explicitly 
to her arguments and to have countered them by presenting newly available 
comparative data and other evidence in support of the ring’s authenticity, 
thereby contradicting several of her earlier observations.[1] Gere’s discussion 
of modernism and Knossos cites a pair of authors who claimed that their ring 
“proves that ‘the so‑called Ring of Nestor, is authentic, and not the fake that 
it was generally believed to be,’” indicating that some scholars have moved from
skepticism toward acceptance of the ring as genuine.[11] These contributions 
collectively demonstrate that opinion on the ring’s authenticity remains 
divided, with sustained arguments both for and against its genuineness.[1][11]',
'source_ids': ['20260628003951047343_67076a76', 
'20260628004021325769_87368900'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Hudler uses 
the history of these changing assessments—Evans’s acceptance, Nilsson’s initial 
use and later suspicion, Biesantz’s negative judgment, Xenaki‑Sakellariou’s 
detailed critique, and Pini’s defense—to illustrate that interpretations of 
artefacts like the Ring of Nestor are historically contingent and shaped by 
changing scholarly positions and social dynamics, rather than solely by material
evidence.[1] In this framework, the Ring of Nestor becomes a paradigmatic 
example of *gemmae dubitandae*, sealstones and rings whose provenance and dating
are uncertain but whose iconography continues to inform discussions of Aegean 
religion and art.[1]', 'source_ids': ['20260628003951047343_67076a76'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Iconographic studies also contribute to scholarly 
interpretations of the ring’s meaning. Eliopoulos’s article “Some Observations 
on the Iconography of the ‘Ring of Nestor,’” published in *ASAtene* 91 
(2013/2015) on pages 209–228, analyzes the ring’s imagery and explores its 
authenticity through iconographic comparison with other Aegean 
artefacts.[2][29][30] Hudler notes that debates about the ring’s authenticity 
have “primarily” relied on observations drawn from this iconographic 
study.[1][2] Vlachopoulos’s chapter “The Ring of Nestor and the Quest for 
Authenticity,” in the volume *Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean 
Iconography*, explicitly cites Eliopoulos’s article and discusses the ring’s 
authenticity within the broader field of Aegean iconography.[28][31]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628003951064180_4a29628f', '20260628004925233210_dce33685',
'20260628004934076549_c8303ca2', '20260628003951047343_67076a76', 
'20260628004916760676_d08416e9', '20260628004935379106_4118c2e3'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Palmer’s thesis on religious motifs in Late Bronze Age 
Aegean glyptic treats the Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, 277) as one of several 
problematic pieces and notes that both it and the Ashmolean ring AM 1938.1110 
have been denounced in modern scholarship.[8] Palmer argues that, given the 
ring’s alleged mainland provenance, its imagery should not be interpreted as 
directly depicting Neopalatial Cretan rituals, and instead considers how its 
scene may reflect the adoption and adaptation of Minoan cult symbols in 
Mycenaean religious practice.[8][15] Studies of composite creatures on seals 
include the Ring of Nestor’s “Minoan dragon” motif when analyzing hybrid beings 
in Aegean art, suggesting that the ring contributes to broader discussions of 
how composite creatures function within cultic imagery.[9]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628004021247324_ba69cb65', '20260628004040270204_142efbb2', 
'20260628004021277210_78e5b375'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'De Angelis’s 
article on epiphany narratives in Aegean glyptic notes that, “because of 
lingering skepticism, neither [the Isopata ring] nor the ring of Nestor” is 
fully accepted despite being considered authentic works by some scholars, 
underlining that debates over authenticity continue to shape how the Ring of 
Nestor is integrated into analyses of epiphany and cult scenes.[7] This 
observation confirms that even where specific authors treat the ring as genuine,
its contested status remains a factor in interpretive work.[1][2][7][28]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628004021222852_03fa4b94', '20260628003951047343_67076a76',
'20260628003951064180_4a29628f', '20260628004916760676_d08416e9'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Hagen Biesantz, Agni Xenaki‑Sakellariou, Ingo Pini, A. 
G. Vlachopoulos, Eliopoulos, Hudler, Nilsson, and others thus represent a 
spectrum of scholarly views: some question the ring’s authenticity on stylistic,
typological, or technical grounds; some regard it as a suspect but still 
informative artefact; and others argue that new comparative material supports 
its authenticity while still acknowledging uncertainties about its provenance 
and dating.[1][2][8][11][28][30][31]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003951047343_67076a76', '20260628003951064180_4a29628f', 
'20260628004021325769_87368900', '20260628004021247324_ba69cb65', 
'20260628004916760676_d08416e9', '20260628004934076549_c8303ca2', 
'20260628004935379106_4118c2e3'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}], 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628003951806867_7d3ed7f5', 
'20260628004022645094_27430b01'], 'section_type': 'analysis'}, {'title': 
'Reception and impact', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor has had a 
sustained impact on Aegean scholarship as an illustrative case in debates over 
forgeries, authenticity, and the methodological treatment of artefacts lacking 
secure provenance. Hudler explicitly identifies it as a paradigmatic example of 
*gemmae dubitandae* and uses the sequence of changing scholarly opinions—from 
Evans’s acceptance through Nilsson’s reclassification as a suspect object, 
Biesantz’s rejection, Xenaki‑Sakellariou’s critique, and Pini’s defense—to argue
that interpretations of such artefacts are historically contingent and shaped by
scholarly and social contexts.[1] In this way, the ring functions not only as a 
piece of glyptic art but also as a reference point in meta‑discussions about 
archaeological argumentation and the evidential status of problematic 
