whose iconography includes cult imagery and a composite creature that has been 
described as a “Minoan dragon,” and it has been repeatedly cited in discussions 
of Minoan–Mycenaean religion and the adoption of Minoan cult symbols on the 
Greek mainland.[8][9][12][14][15] The object’s authenticity and provenance are 
disputed: Evans accepted it as genuine, while some later scholars classified it 
among suspect or possibly forged artefacts, and other researchers have argued in
favor of its authenticity on the basis of new comparative material and 
iconographic analysis.[1][2][7][10][11][26][28][30][31] Because the Ring of 
